WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.488
The most consequential boundary decisions
don't look like boundary decisions.

2
00:00:04.488 --> 00:00:06.243
They look like engineering tickets,

3
00:00:06.243 --> 00:00:09.112
and that's how they slip past
the people who should make them.

4
00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:09.963
Placement.

5
00:00:09.963 --> 00:00:14.401
Vending a new subscription is the
platform team's mechanism, but whether

6
00:00:14.401 --> 00:00:18.913
a workload is inside the authorized
system is the system owner's decision.

7
00:00:18.913 --> 00:00:22.123
The cheapest way to turn
an authorized system into

8
00:00:22.123 --> 00:00:25.563
an unauthorized one is a
subscription no one recorded.

9
00:00:25.563 --> 00:00:29.974
A workload whose subscription isn't in
the boundary record doesn't go live.

10
00:00:29.974 --> 00:00:31.263
Feature dependency.

11
00:00:31.263 --> 00:00:35.012
The system owner checks each
feature a design depends on against

12
00:00:35.012 --> 00:00:38.762
what's been tested to work in your
cloud, before the design is built.

13
00:00:38.762 --> 00:00:39.850
The contract.

14
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:44.799
The GCC listing says customers must
buy a GCC SKU to be covered by the GCC

15
00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:48.826
authorization, so a licensing
choice is a boundary choice,

16
00:00:48.826 --> 00:00:51.762
and it belongs to the contracting officer.

17
00:00:51.762 --> 00:00:53.075
Provider changes.

18
00:00:53.075 --> 00:00:57.381
When a provider notice might change
the agency's risk, the authorizing

19
00:00:57.381 --> 00:01:01.612
official decides, not whoever happens
to be on the distribution list.

20
00:01:01.612 --> 00:01:02.887
And asking for more.

21
00:01:02.887 --> 00:01:08.257
Agencies must not require a certified
service to supply more than FedRAMP requires,

22
00:01:08.257 --> 00:01:13.626
unless the agency head or a delegate finds
a demonstrable need and notifies FedRAMP.

23
00:01:13.626 --> 00:01:17.700
The landing zone makes these decisions
checkable, but it doesn't make them.

24
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.538
It enforces structure, and its
compliance reports are evidence,

25
00:01:21.538 --> 00:01:25.375
yet Microsoft itself calls the
policy compliance view partial.

26
00:01:25.375 --> 00:01:27.300
Two planning facts for leaders.

27
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:31.713
Microsoft's landing-zone accelerators
are supported only in public Azure,

28
00:01:31.713 --> 00:01:35.200
so an Azure Government build
needs the code adapted by hand.

29
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.255
And the pipeline that builds it is
inside the boundary question too,

30
00:01:39.255 --> 00:01:41.862
because Terraform state is sensitive data.

31
00:01:41.862 --> 00:01:44.090
Plan both; don't discover them.
